ted people so  raise your hands if you think more  educated people are more supportive okay  unless educated great so I'm very happy  that the room was it didn't have  consensus because then I can say that  there's value in the science that I'm  about to present so we will learn  something so the answer is the sort of  prototypical person who is more opposed  to AI is female less educated poor and  without computer science background or  experience and interestingly this is for  both AI and a and HL mi it correlates  pretty strongly some other less strong  results related to religion and  political partisanship for example so  this is one issue that hasn't broken yet  on partisan grounds though might  speaking to a point max made this  morning if we ask them about how sort of  hopeful they are about HTMI in this case  we asked them for the expected impact of  a chiller by you'll see twelve percent  chose extremely bad possibly human  extinction that's more more than double  the proportion that's said extremely  good so and then also unbalanced bad  outweighs ever so slightly unbalanced  good so Americans at least are not  thinking that AGI is going to bring  utopia or they're not convinced of that  and this is important for a few reasons  one these might be legitimate concerns  that it would be good to address but to  this level of sort of negative  expectation about what AGI means for for  them and for Humanity could again lead  to that backlash could lead to  resistance to all the possibilities in  the up side that we see so I think this  is something we should you know be  taking very seriously  okay so there's number of other issues  again I'm just going to jump in a report  from early 2018 was the malicious use of  AI report which many of you may have  seen looking at the many ways that AI  enables new sort of malicious actions  again it's not to say that AI is net  negative but it's worth being attentive  to those possibilities  another new paper  by Nick Bostrom is the vulnerable world  hypothesis which asks if technology  becomes especially destructive so if one  person could cause a lot of harm  because enabled by new technology what  does that mean for world order and for  sort of the ideal governance among these  many issues I think there's an  underlying issue which I and others  focus on which is competition between  firms countries and especially great  power security competition I see this as  an amplifier and something that  exacerbates many of these other issues  and even issues like privacy which you  might think is an issue that a country  can figure out for itself right a  country well governed country say Sweden  should be able to formulate its own  optimal policies with respect to privacy  what competition puts constraints or at  least it forces on them trade-offs that  they might not want to make for example  the EU I might want to have stronger  privacy legislation but if they do that  they're worried that they will have  difficulties cultivating new AI  companies and AI champions so now they  face this trade-off between economic  prosperity and there are sort of  preferred values and how algorithms are  deployed and this I think applies to  almost all of these issues that  competition between countries and  especially competition that touches on  security national security concerns  makes it much harder to solve these  problems I want to also complicate two  metaphors that we often use for  understanding risks from AI so we often  talk about them as coming from accidents  or coming from misuse right so an  accident is something that arises  because the engineers weren't careful  enough or they didn't spend enough time  looking at the system or maybe the  science was insufficiently developed or  maybe the the machinery is very  complicated and so it's accident-prone  whereas misuse is the kind of harm that  comes from some malicious actor usually  a rogue actor a terrorist or criminal  who gets their hands on the technology  and then deploys it in a way that's  against society's preferences these two  perspectives are really useful but  they're incomplete and in fact I think  they miss a lot of the variation and  where risk comes from and also a lot of  the opportunities for policy  intervention so Remco and I suggest a  structural perspective which focuses on  these various structural prop  of society politics the economy that are  more likely to generate risks and I have  here John F Kennedy during the Cuban  Missile Crisis as the sort of exemplary  image because I don't think we would  call the Cuban Missile Crisis an  accident right it's not that the nuclear  weapons systems were poorly built  leading to the crisis  nor was it a case of misuse it wasn't a  terrorist or some rogue actor outside  the sort of legitimate political order  that led to the crisis rather it was the  legitimate leaders of two great powers  who found themselves at the brink of  nuclear war because of structural  strategic properties of the  international system so I think a lot of  our thinking has to be much more  structural to really grasp where the  risks come from we're looking at the  levers of influence that the US  government for example would have over  AI companies in the industry and vice  versa  to try and understand what the strategic  game could look like in the future we're  also looking at historical examples of  attempts to control powerful technology  like in this case depicted the  conversations after World War two to  control nuclear weapons so many of us  weren't aware of this there was a  serious conversation that took place  about the u.s. giving up nuclear weapons  to the UN in order for there not to be  an arms race in nuclear weapons this  conversation did not succeed but there's  a lot of interesting lessons about how  that took place including number one  that scientists can play an extremely  important role politically and also  enabling cooperation such as by finding  technical possibilities that would  otherwise be overlooked okay so moving  on to ideal governance in many ways this  is the work that I think's been least  done so far and I applaud Max for in  both his book but also at the two  conferences Asilomar and here really  trying to have us articulate what are  these positive visions that we can look  towards some work that we've done at FHI  is this paper policy to zero to zero  rata for super intelligent AI where we  tried to articulate what are things that  are what are sort of features that  become more important in a world of  advanced machine intelligence and some  other work is again from the survey that  is going to be public in a few days  where we asked Americans what are the  governance challenges that they regarded  as most important and most likely and  the takeaways here are one they regard  them all  pretty important so the bottom of this  figure is 2.5 that's somewhere between  somewhat important and very important  and the other takeaways that there is  variation and how Americans perceive  these governance challenges data privacy  cyber tax and surveillance standout is  among the more salient to them and  finally it's good to do this work this  research but it's even better if we can  translate that into policy  recommendations for the near term so  I'll tell you about one this is called  the windfall clause this was first  articulated in Nick's book and the idea  is if some actor some company wins the  AI windfall wins the AI lottery right  they become this super corporation with  the best AGI that's capturing all the  markets it would be good if they  redistributed a lot of that wealth to  the world to humanity and the idea  behind this proposal is that it could be  a legally committing obligation that  they would voluntarily undertake now  before when they're far away from this  windfall to give away the bounty in the  event that they happen to win the  lottery and what's really exciting is  that legally it seems to be permissible  under Delaware law to make this  contractual commitment so long as the  expected costs at the time of the  commitment are less than ten percent of  national annual taxable income so the  basic idea is as long as that you're not  giving away too much right away and  you're not but if the risk is  sufficiently high that you're gonna win  or the probability sufficiently high or  low that you're gonna win then it's  tolerable to shareholder to share value  I mean shareholders don't have a legal  basis to sue against that so in summary  there's a lot of important questions and  work to be done and but what's great is  there's a lot of talent that's pouring  into this field working on these  projects so I look forward to in the  coming years working with many of you to  answer these questions Thanks  [Applause]  [Music]
 

